  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "Why with Exists",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Leak Problem",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Files, locks, sockets, DB conns must be released",
            "Release must happen even when the code raises",
            "A forgotten release leaks the OS handle",
            "Leaked handles pile up until the process dies"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "f = open('x')",
            "data = f.read()   # raises -> jumps out",
            "f.close()         # never reached"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "try/finally: The Manual Fix"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "f = open('x')",
            "try:",
            "    data = f.read()",
            "finally:",
            "    f.close()     # runs on every exit"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Correct, but four lines of ceremony per resource",
            "Repeated verbatim at every single call site",
            "Easy to drop the finally under deadline"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "with = Packaged try/finally",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A context manager is reusable try/finally, one keyword",
            "`with open('x') as f:` always closes f",
            "Closes on normal exit and on exception",
            "Closes on return, break and continue too"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "RAII, Python Style",
          "items": [
            "C++: destructor fires at scope exit",
            "Python locals have no deterministic destruction",
            "`with` is the explicit stand-in"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Never Trust Refcounts",
          "text": "CPython often closes a dropped file at once. That is an implementation detail, not a promise — PyPy and Jython do not. Never rely on it."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`ResourceWarning: unclosed file <_io.TextIOWrapper ...>`",
            "That warning means a `with` was missing",
            "Run tests with -W error to make it loud"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "try:",
            "    VAR = value    # bind is inside the try",
            "    BODY",
            "except BaseException:",
            "    if not exit(mgr, *sys.exc_info()): raise"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Not Only Resources",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Managers guard state PAIRS, not just handles",
            "set/restore, enter/leave, begin/commit",
            "Any paired action belongs in a manager"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Origin and Grammar"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "PEP 343 added `with` in Python 2.5",
            "2.5 needed `from __future__ import with_statement`",
            "Standard, no import, from 2.6 onward",
            "`with EXPR as VAR:` — EXPR must be a manager",
            "VAR binds __enter__'s RETURN, not EXPR itself",
            "`as VAR` is optional: `with lock:` is valid"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "with open('a') as a, open('b') as b:",
            "    pass   # enter L->R, exit R->L"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Why with Exists (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Comma Form == Nesting",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "with open('a') as a:",
            "    with open('b') as b:   # identical",
            "        pass               # to a, b"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Comma form loses no exception safety",
            "If the second open() fails, the first still closes",
            "`with a, b:` evaluates b only after a.__enter__()"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Parenthesized Form (3.10+)"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "with (",
            "    open('a') as a,",
            "    open('b') as b,   # trailing comma OK",
            "):",
            "    pass"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The as Target"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "VAR is a normal assignment target",
            "Tuple unpacking works: `with mgr() as (x, y):`",
            "VAR is NOT scoped to the block — it survives"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Survives, But Dead",
          "text": "After `with open(p) as f:` the name f still exists. The file behind it is closed. Touching it raises ValueError."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Statement, Not Expression"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Cannot be used inline in a comprehension or lambda",
            "`return` inside `with` runs __exit__ first",
            "Then the function actually returns"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def read(p):",
            "    with open(p) as f:",
            "        return f.read()   # close runs first"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Protocol",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Manager = any object with __enter__ and __exit__",
            "`__exit__(self, exc_type, exc_value, traceback)`",
            "Both looked up on the TYPE, not the instance",
            "`obj.__enter__ = f` does nothing — special-method lookup"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "class Seven:",
            "    def __enter__(self): return 7",
            "    def __exit__(self, *a): return None",
            "with Seven() as v:",
            "    print(v)  # 7"
          ],
          "runnable": true
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Path",
            "__exit__ args",
            "Return"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "clean exit",
              "(None, None, None)",
              "ignored"
            ],
            [
              "body raised",
              "live exc triple",
              "truthy = suppress"
            ],
            [
              "body raised",
              "live exc triple",
              "None/False = propagate"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "The return True Bug",
          "text": "A habitual `return True` at the end of __exit__ swallows every exception in the block. Return nothing instead."
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Syntax, Semantics, Protocol",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Minimal Real Manager",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "class Timer:",
            "    def __enter__(self):",
            "        self.t = time.perf_counter(); return self",
            "    def __exit__(self, *exc):",
            "        print(time.perf_counter() - self.t)"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "__enter__ returning self is the common choice",
            "__exit__ also runs for GeneratorExit, KeyboardInterrupt",
            "And SystemExit — all BaseException subclasses",
            "So cleanup must be fast and must not throw"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Typing the Protocol"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def __exit__(self,",
            "    exc_type: type[BaseException] | None,",
            "    exc: BaseException | None,",
            "    tb: TracebackType | None,",
            ") -> bool | None: ..."
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`contextlib.AbstractContextManager` is the ABC",
            "It supplies __enter__ returning self",
            "You still must define __exit__"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Worked Example: The ABC",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "class Quiet(AbstractContextManager):",
            "    def __exit__(self, *exc):",
            "        return False   # never suppress"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "No __enter__ written — the ABC returns self",
            "isinstance() checks work against the ABC",
            "Explicit False documents the no-suppress choice"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Raising Inside __exit__",
          "text": "A new exception from __exit__ replaces the original. The original survives as __context__ in the chained traceback — noisy, and easy to miss."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What with Compiles To",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "mgr = EXPR",
            "exit = type(mgr).__exit__   # fetched FIRST",
            "value = type(mgr).__enter__(mgr)"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Syntax, Semantics, Protocol (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Clean path instead calls exit(mgr, None, None, None)",
            "__exit__ fetched before __enter__ runs",
            "A manager missing __exit__ fails before any setup"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "__enter__ Raises = No __exit__",
          "text": "If __enter__ blows up, __exit__ is NEVER called. Setup must be all-or-nothing inside __enter__."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Bytecode Names"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Version",
            "Setup op",
            "Error op"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "3.11+",
              "BEFORE_WITH",
              "WITH_EXCEPT_START"
            ],
            [
              "<=3.10",
              "SETUP_WITH",
              "WITH_CLEANUP_START"
            ],
            [
              "<=3.10",
              "—",
              "WITH_CLEANUP_FINISH"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "import dis",
            "src = 'with a as b: pass'",
            "dis.dis(compile(src, '<s>', 'exec'))"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "BEFORE_WITH pushes the exit callable + enter result",
            "Manager stays on the stack for the whole block",
            "So it cannot be garbage collected mid-body"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What with Costs",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The `as` unpack happens inside the try",
            "A failing tuple unpack still fires __exit__",
            "Cost: one attribute lookup + two calls",
            "Negligible against I/O; measurable in tight loops"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Class Skeleton"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "class Res:",
            "    def __init__(self, cfg): self.cfg = cfg",
            "    def __enter__(self): self.h = acquire(self.cfg); return self.h",
            "    def __exit__(self, *exc): self.h.release()"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "__init__ stores config only",
            "__enter__ acquires and returns the resource",
            "Acquiring in __enter__ keeps the object reusable"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Example: Scoped chdir",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def __enter__(self):",
            "    self.old = os.getcwd()",
            "    os.chdir(self.path)",
            "def __exit__(self, *exc):",
            "    os.chdir(self.old)     # always restored"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Example: Transaction"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def __exit__(self, exc_type, exc, tb):",
            "    if exc_type is None: self.conn.commit()",
            "    else: self.conn.rollback()",
            "    return None            # let it propagate"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Manager",
            "Reusable?",
            "Notes"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "threading.Lock",
              "yes",
              "re-enter after exit"
            ],
            [
              "class you write",
              "by design",
              "if state is not saved flat"
            ],
            [
              "@contextmanager",
              "no",
              "single-use generator"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "__exit__ Rules and Desugaring",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Most managers are NOT reentrant",
            "Entering one instance twice corrupts saved state",
            "Need it? push saved state onto a stack"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Retrofits and Helpers",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Bolt __enter__/__exit__ onto an existing class",
            "Its public API does not change; it becomes with-able",
            "Subclass ContextDecorator to also use it as @mgr"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "from contextlib import closing",
            "with closing(urlopen(url)) as page:",
            "    body = page.read()   # any .close() object"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "__exit__ Hygiene",
          "text": "Make release idempotent so a double close cannot raise. Log the exception if you like, but return None so it re-raises — never silently drop it."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Generator Shortcut",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "@contextmanager",
            "def timed(label):",
            "    t = perf_counter()",
            "    try: yield",
            "    finally: print(label, perf_counter() - t)"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Setup, then `yield value`, then teardown",
            "The yielded value is what `as` binds",
            "No class, no __enter__, no __exit__"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Exactly One Yield"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Yields",
            "Result"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "0",
              "RuntimeError: generator didn't yield"
            ],
            [
              "1",
              "correct manager"
            ],
            [
              "2+",
              "RuntimeError: generator didn't stop"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Errors Enter the Generator",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A body exception is thrown in at the yield",
            "Mechanically that is gen.throw()",
            "So a bare yield SKIPS teardown when the body raises"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "yield x",
            "cleanup()   # BUG: skipped on exception",
            "# fix:",
            "try: yield x",
            "finally: cleanup()"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Suppressing Here Is Different"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "try:",
            "    yield",
            "except ValueError:",
            "    return      # swallowed; True does nothing"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Single-Use, Also A Decorator"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "One instance = one entry; a second raises RuntimeError",
            "Call the factory again for a fresh generator",
            "Results inherit ContextDecorator: `@timed('run')` works",
            "Each decorated call builds its own generator"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Cost, and Class Managers",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "Wins on",
            "Loses on"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "@contextmanager",
              "short, linear read",
              "single-use"
            ],
            [
              "class",
              "reuse, reentrancy",
              "more code"
            ],
            [
              "class",
              "subclass, introspect",
              "—"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "nullcontext",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "cm = open(p) if p else nullcontext(sys.stdout)",
            "with cm as out:",
            "    out.write('hi')   # one code path"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`nullcontext(x)` binds x and does nothing else",
            "Call it bare when no value is needed",
            "Keeps the `if` out of the block body"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Worked Example: Optional Lock",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "lock = Lock() if shared else nullcontext()",
            "with lock:",
            "    counter += 1   # both modes, one block"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "No duplicated body per branch",
            "Swap the manager, not the logic"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "suppress()"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "from contextlib import suppress",
            "with suppress(FileNotFoundError):",
            "    os.remove(path)   # gone already? fine"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Replaces `try/except X: pass` boilerplate",
            "Intent is clearer than a bare except",
            "Takes several types: `suppress(KeyError, IndexError)`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Suppression Aborts The Block",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "from contextlib import suppress",
            "with suppress(ZeroDivisionError):",
            "    1 / 0",
            "    print('skipped')",
            "print('after')  # after"
          ],
          "runnable": true
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Statements after the raising line do NOT run",
            "It is an exit, not a `continue`",
            "It cannot catch anything raised outside its block",
            "And it never retries"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Subclasses Count Too"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`suppress(OSError)` also eats FileNotFoundError",
            "And PermissionError, and every other OSError child",
            "Never write `suppress(Exception)` as a catch-all",
            "It hides real bugs and makes debugging opaque"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "How It Works"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def __exit__(self, exc_type, exc, tb):",
            "    return (exc_type is not None",
            "        and issubclass(exc_type, self._exceptions))"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Cost, and Class Managers (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Reentrant and reusable — no generator inside",
            "Safe to keep one instance as a module constant",
            "3.12+: it also matches inside an ExceptionGroup",
            "Unmatched members of the group are re-raised"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Need",
            "Use"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "log or recover",
              "try/except"
            ],
            [
              "nothing to do",
              "suppress"
            ],
            [
              "retry",
              "neither — write a loop"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Worked Example: Best-Effort",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "for p in paths:",
            "    with suppress(FileNotFoundError):",
            "        os.remove(p)   # loop keeps going"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Suppression ends the block, not the loop",
            "Each iteration re-enters the same manager",
            "One statement per block is the goal"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Keep The Block Tiny",
          "text": "Every extra line inside `suppress` is another error you did not mean to discard. Wrap the risky call and nothing else."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Why ExitStack Exists",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The manager count is unknown until runtime",
            "You cannot write a fixed `with a, b, c:`",
            "ExitStack collects managers as you go"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "from contextlib import ExitStack",
            "with ExitStack() as stack:",
            "    fs = [stack.enter_context(open(p))",
            "          for p in paths]   # all closed, LIFO"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Four Registrations",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Call",
            "Registers",
            "Returns"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "enter_context(cm)",
              "cm.__exit__",
              "__enter__ value"
            ],
            [
              "callback(fn, *a)",
              "plain function",
              "fn"
            ],
            [
              "push(exiter)",
              "__exit__-shaped",
              "exiter"
            ],
            [
